---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets"
import CaseStudy from "@/layouts/case-study.astro"
import { getProject } from "@/data/projects"
import verdictPoorImg from "@/assets/projects/wheely-weather/verdict-poor.png"
import weekAheadImg from "@/assets/projects/wheely-weather/week-ahead.png"

const project = getProject("wheely-weather")!
const pictureFormats: ("avif" | "webp")[] = ["avif", "webp"]
const pictureWidths = [640, 960, 1200]
const pictureSizes = "(min-width: 768px) 960px, 100vw"
---

<CaseStudy
  project={project}
  title="Wheely Weather"
  seoDescription="Wheely Weather is a side project, now on the App Store: an Expo app for iOS, Android, and web that turns live weather and air-quality data into a 0–5 star ride verdict, hourly and 8-day ratings, and kit picks."
>
  <h2>Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Wheely Weather answers one core question before a ride:{" "}
    <em>is it a good day to ride?</em> Conversations with recreational and
    commuter cyclists highlighted three weather signals that mattered most: dew
    point, feels-like temperature, and wind speed. Among these, dew point was
    the most critical indicator riders missed in standard weather apps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wheely Weather is live on the{" "}
    <a href={project.appStoreUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      App Store
    </a>
    , and the same app runs on the web at{" "}
    <a href="https://wheelyweather.app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      wheelyweather.app
    </a>
    .
  </p>

  <h2 id="how-it-works">How It Works</h2>
  <p>
    The screen follows the order of a rider’s questions: can I go, why did it
    get that rating, and what should I wear?
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Ride verdict:</strong> a 0–5 star score and a go, maybe, or skip
      call for the best daylight ride window, built from temperature, wind and
      gusts, rain, dew point, air quality, UV, and daylight. The verdict names
      the deciding issues first. Once the light is gone, it looks ahead to
      tomorrow.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Hourly and 8-day ratings:</strong> every hour and day is rated
      Good to Bad with a one-line reason, and the week view highlights the best
      ride day ahead.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kit picks:</strong> what to wear for the conditions, tuned for
      Casual or Roadie riders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Home climate:</strong> comfort limits shift if your home runs hot,
      humid, or cold, more so the more often you ride outdoors.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Alerts and widget:</strong> severe weather and air quality
      warnings, plus an iOS Home Screen widget with the verdict and the next 12
      hours.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Raw weather numbers stay visible for riders who want the details.</p>

  <figure>
    <Picture
      src={verdictPoorImg}
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
      alt="Wheely Weather on iPhone showing a 1.5-star ‘Wrench day’ verdict for Austin, very hot at 93°F, with an ‘After dark: lights on’ note on the hourly chart."
    />
    <figcaption>
      On a bad day the verdict leads with the reason: here, the heat.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <figure>
    <Picture
      src={weekAheadImg}
      formats={pictureFormats}
      widths={pictureWidths}
      sizes={pictureSizes}
      alt="Wheely Weather’s week ahead list for Portland, each day rated Fair or Good, with Monday tagged Best."
    />
    <figcaption>
      The week ahead rates each day and tags the best one to ride.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Built With</h2>
  <p>
    One Expo Router and React Native codebase covers iOS, Android, and web. The
    ride-scoring rules live in a single shared TypeScript module, so every
    platform scores conditions the same way.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>iOS:</strong> released on the{" "}
      <a href={project.appStoreUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        App Store
      </a>
      , built and submitted with EAS. Apple WeatherKit and MapKit run through
      small Swift native modules, and the Home Screen widget is SwiftUI (my
      Swift is basic).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Web:</strong> live on Cloudflare at{" "}
      <a href="https://wheelyweather.app" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        wheelyweather.app
      </a>
      , with a Cloudflare Worker that serves WeatherKit forecasts and falls back
      to Open-Meteo.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data:</strong> Apple WeatherKit on iOS and web, Open-Meteo on
      Android and for air quality everywhere.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Quality:</strong> strict TypeScript, Vitest unit tests with
      coverage floors, Maestro end-to-end flows on iOS, Sentry error reporting,
      and GitHub Actions CI that gates deploys.
    </li>
  </ul>
</CaseStudy>
